1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,474
Two government documents
describe the same sighting.

2
00:00:03,767 --> 00:00:08,183
Federal agents, at a sensitive
site in the western United States,

3
00:00:08,233 --> 00:00:11,804
see a matte black triangle,
up to fifteen feet across.

4
00:00:11,854 --> 00:00:16,584
It rises above a ridge. Hovers.
Then drops into a canyon.

5
00:00:16,896 --> 00:00:19,147
No sound. No exhaust.

6
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:24,930
The first document is a memo from
AARO, the Pentagon's UFO office.

7
00:00:24,980 --> 00:00:28,962
It says the object was in view
for more than thirty seconds.

8
00:00:29,193 --> 00:00:31,524
The second is an FBI write-up.

9
00:00:31,574 --> 00:00:35,722
It says about three, and that the
object looked most like a drone.

10
00:00:36,111 --> 00:00:39,259
Both are official. They
can't both be right.

11
00:00:39,710 --> 00:00:42,368
A search the next morning found nothing.

12
00:00:42,466 --> 00:00:44,102
And nobody filmed it.

13
00:00:44,259 --> 00:00:50,036
Both files came out on October 23rd, in
the Pentagon's seventh batch of UFO files.

14
00:00:50,209 --> 00:00:53,145
To see why a case with no video
still gets taken seriously,

15
00:00:53,195 --> 00:00:59,083
you have to go back twenty-two years.
To the footage everyone has seen...

16
00:00:59,441 --> 00:01:01,012
and what it doesn't show.

17
00:01:01,360 --> 00:01:04,820
This is real footage from
a US Navy fighter jet.

18
00:01:04,870 --> 00:01:07,193
The Pentagon released it officially.

19
00:01:07,346 --> 00:01:10,288
And to this day, it calls
what you're looking at...

20
00:01:10,537 --> 00:01:11,918
unidentified.

21
00:01:12,260 --> 00:01:15,129
It's known as FLIR1.

22
00:01:15,353 --> 00:01:18,507
It was recorded by a weapons systems
officer named Chad Underwood,

23
00:01:18,557 --> 00:01:22,836
with an infrared camera
locked onto a distant target.

24
00:01:23,086 --> 00:01:25,934
At the end, the object slips out of frame.

25
00:01:26,120 --> 00:01:31,712
Some see a sudden burst of speed. Skeptics
say the camera simply lost its lock.

26
00:01:31,880 --> 00:01:34,296
The footage alone can't settle it.

27
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:39,022
But this video isn't the strange
part. Earlier that same day,

28
00:01:39,072 --> 00:01:43,272
two Navy jets flew down to look at
this object with their own eyes.

29
00:01:43,473 --> 00:01:46,662
One of those pilots later
told Congress, under oath,

30
00:01:46,712 --> 00:01:49,964
that it was far superior
to anything we have.

31
00:01:50,193 --> 00:01:52,049
This is what they described.

32
00:01:55,800 --> 00:01:57,867
November, 2004.

33
00:01:58,159 --> 00:02:03,096
The aircraft carrier USS Nimitz is training
off the coast of Southern California.

34
00:02:03,146 --> 00:02:07,206
Its escort, the cruiser USS
Princeton, has a problem.

35
00:02:07,256 --> 00:02:13,202
For more than two weeks, its radar has been tracking
objects that appear above eighty thousand feet,

36
00:02:13,252 --> 00:02:16,020
then drop to around twenty thousand.

37
00:02:16,400 --> 00:02:19,381
On November fourteenth,
Commander David Fravor,

38
00:02:19,431 --> 00:02:23,818
who leads the Black Aces fighter
squadron, takes off for a training fight.

39
00:02:23,998 --> 00:02:28,290
Two F/A-18F Super Hornets,
two people in each.

40
00:02:28,450 --> 00:02:31,358
Flying the other jet is
Lieutenant Alex Dietrich.

41
00:02:31,569 --> 00:02:35,889
Mid-flight, the exercise is called
off. There's something out there.

42
00:02:35,939 --> 00:02:38,327
And they are the closest jets.

43
00:02:38,900 --> 00:02:44,238
The sea is calm. Clear sky,
light wind, no waves breaking.

44
00:02:44,533 --> 00:02:46,670
Then they notice whitewater.

45
00:02:46,929 --> 00:02:51,292
A patch of churning sea, as if
something is just below the surface.

46
00:02:51,416 --> 00:02:53,822
And above it, moving
abruptly over the water,

47
00:02:53,872 --> 00:02:58,332
is what Fravor later described
as a white Tic Tac object.

48
00:02:58,514 --> 00:03:02,882
No wings. No rotors. Nothing
he could see holding it up.

49
00:03:03,100 --> 00:03:07,492
Fravor drops down for a closer
look. And the object responds.

50
00:03:07,586 --> 00:03:12,154
In his words, it aligned itself with
his aircraft, and began to climb.

51
00:03:12,336 --> 00:03:18,190
He cuts across to meet it. Then, he says,
it rapidly accelerated in front of them...

52
00:03:18,387 --> 00:03:21,574
and disappeared. They turn
back to the whitewater.

53
00:03:21,761 --> 00:03:23,086
That's gone too.

54
00:03:24,300 --> 00:03:27,818
Then the radio. The ship
had found it again.

55
00:03:27,868 --> 00:03:31,578
At their planned meeting point.
Roughly sixty miles away.

56
00:03:31,628 --> 00:03:33,306
In less than a minute.

57
00:03:34,490 --> 00:03:37,965
Sixty miles in under a minute.
That's a mile every second.

58
00:03:38,015 --> 00:03:42,240
At least thirty-six hundred miles an hour.

59
00:03:42,496 --> 00:03:45,791
But there's a catch. That number came
over the radio, from the ship's radar.

60
00:03:45,841 --> 00:03:49,551
There's no recording of it you can watch.

61
00:03:49,601 --> 00:03:52,183
So it's only as good as the radar track.

62
00:03:52,321 --> 00:03:55,965
Which makes the radar either the
strongest evidence in this case...

63
00:03:56,015 --> 00:03:58,301
or the first thing to question.

64
00:04:01,850 --> 00:04:05,194
Here's what sets this
case apart. Two jets.

65
00:04:05,306 --> 00:04:10,862
Two people in each. A pilot up front, and
behind them a weapons systems officer,

66
00:04:10,912 --> 00:04:14,040
whose whole job is working
the radar and sensors.

67
00:04:14,257 --> 00:04:18,766
Fravor's was Jim Slaight. Dietrich's
has never been publicly named.

68
00:04:19,065 --> 00:04:22,522
Fravor says all four of them
looked down and saw it.

69
00:04:22,572 --> 00:04:26,770
For about five minutes, from two
cockpits, at different angles.

70
00:04:26,988 --> 00:04:29,774
Four trained people can
still be wrong together.

71
00:04:29,824 --> 00:04:33,342
That happens. But they weren't
the only ones looking.

72
00:04:33,900 --> 00:04:39,074
On the Princeton, the radar operator who
sent them there was Senior Chief Kevin Day.

73
00:04:39,301 --> 00:04:43,352
He says that for days, he'd been
watching groups of objects on his radar,

74
00:04:43,402 --> 00:04:46,589
sometimes ten at a time. Some, he says,

75
00:04:46,639 --> 00:04:51,047
dropped from twenty-eight thousand
feet toward the sea in under a second.

76
00:04:51,295 --> 00:04:55,824
Eyes in two cockpits, and a warship's
radar, all on the same thing.

77
00:04:56,123 --> 00:04:59,101
The one piece nobody outside
the Navy has seen...

78
00:04:59,419 --> 00:05:01,552
is the raw radar data.

79
00:05:05,300 --> 00:05:08,038
So what does a video prove on its own?

80
00:05:08,335 --> 00:05:12,670
There's another Navy clip that shows
exactly why that question matters.

81
00:05:16,020 --> 00:05:23,274
This one is called Go Fast. A Navy jet,
off the coast of Florida, January 2015.

82
00:05:23,582 --> 00:05:27,342
It looks like something skimming
the ocean at incredible speed.

83
00:05:27,392 --> 00:05:33,900
And the crew are clearly excited. But in
2025, the Pentagon's UFO office, AARO,

84
00:05:33,950 --> 00:05:37,800
ran the numbers from the
camera's own readouts.

85
00:05:37,850 --> 00:05:41,217
The object was about
thirteen thousand feet up.

86
00:05:41,368 --> 00:05:46,039
Nowhere near the water. The sea
rushing past is the jet's own motion.

87
00:05:46,369 --> 00:05:51,311
AARO's estimate? Somewhere between
five and ninety-two miles an hour.

88
00:05:51,406 --> 00:05:52,894
Moving with the wind.

89
00:05:53,162 --> 00:05:58,498
In their words: high confidence that the
object did not move at anomalous speeds.

90
00:05:59,220 --> 00:06:03,818
So a video can fool you. The
Nimitz case never rested on one.

91
00:06:04,048 --> 00:06:09,954
It rests on people and radar. The famous
video came later, and shows something else.

92
00:06:13,500 --> 00:06:18,219
And twenty years later...
the reports haven't stopped.

93
00:06:18,269 --> 00:06:20,376
Pilots. Sailors. Drone crews.

94
00:06:20,543 --> 00:06:26,662
In just over a year, the Pentagon's
UFO office logged 757 new reports.

95
00:06:26,712 --> 00:06:29,220
Most of them never reach the public.

96
00:06:29,300 --> 00:06:31,305
In February 2026,

97
00:06:31,355 --> 00:06:37,210
the President directed the Pentagon and other
agencies to start releasing their files on UFOs.

98
00:06:37,417 --> 00:06:43,569
Since May, the Pentagon has published
seven batches, at war.gov/UFO.

99
00:06:43,620 --> 00:06:47,693
The latest, on October
23rd, was documents only.

100
00:06:47,795 --> 00:06:48,995
No new video.

101
00:06:49,670 --> 00:06:52,169
Which brings us back to
that black triangle.

102
00:06:52,402 --> 00:06:56,192
The AARO memo says more
than thirty seconds.

103
00:06:56,336 --> 00:06:58,581
The FBI says about three.

104
00:06:58,790 --> 00:07:02,424
Two reports from the same
government, about the same sighting.

105
00:07:02,474 --> 00:07:04,914
And no footage to check them against.

106
00:07:08,460 --> 00:07:13,042
A Tic Tac over the Pacific. A
black triangle over a ridge.

107
00:07:13,218 --> 00:07:17,697
Different decades, same problem.
Enough to take seriously.

108
00:07:17,824 --> 00:07:19,331
Not enough to close.

109
00:07:20,280 --> 00:07:24,827
Twenty-two years on, nobody has
officially explained the Tic Tac.

110
00:07:25,113 --> 00:07:28,366
Alex Dietrich, who flew the
other jet, put it simply.

111
00:07:28,602 --> 00:07:32,007
We don't know what it was.
That's not proof of aliens.

112
00:07:32,127 --> 00:07:36,740
But it isn't explained, either. Four
people saw something they couldn't name.

113
00:07:36,874 --> 00:07:39,067
And nobody has named it since.

114
00:07:39,419 --> 00:07:41,791
If you want the next file the
moment it drops, subscribe.

115
00:07:41,841 --> 00:07:45,303
I'll keep reading them,
so you don't have to.
